  data model on the fly.

  Now the question that actually decides the design, and it bites hardest in a single-user app:

  [1mThe fork:[0m "tasks they care about" is doing a lot of work in your sentence. With one user and one browser, [3myou[0m are the
  only person who changes anything — so a naive system notifies you about your own edits, which is noise. What counts as
  "care about" determines whether this feature is useful or annoying.

  - [1mExplicit watching[0m — you star/watch specific tasks, only those notify. Predictable, zero false positives, but it's
    manual and you'll forget to star things. Cheap to add rules on top later.
  - [1mTime-based[0m — notifications come from due dates and staleness, not edits at all. Genuinely useful solo, and needs no
    subscription model. Narrower: says nothing when content changes.
  - [1mRule-based[0m — you define conditions (tag, priority, overdue) and matching tasks notify. Most flexible, most to build,
    and needs a rule editor UI.
  - [1mIt's actually multi-user[0m — if "users" plural was literal, the whole substrate recommendation above is wrong and we
    should back up.

  [1mMy recommendation: time-based, with explicit watching as the extension point.[0m In a solo app, due dates and staleness
  are the only changes you genuinely don't already know about, and it needs no subscription plumbing at all. Watching is
  a clean addition once the event stream exists.
